  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7b8860589b05b4642ab42b24201a782d, type: 3}
  m_Name: Book_HowToCan
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::GrandmasHouse.Data.NoteData
  kind: 3
  title: How to Can
  recipe: {fileID: 0}
  body: 
  marginNote: 
  pages:
  - 'Two recipes are in this book: dill pickles, and pickled garlic. Both take an
    afternoon and fill the pantry for winter.


    For dill pickles you will need:
    cucumbers, a head of dill and two cloves of garlic for each pint jar, vinegar
    and salt from the pantry, pint jars with lids, the cooking pot, and the big water-bath
    canner.


    A canner holds four pints, so plan your batch in fours.'
  - 'Getting ready


    Lay a cucumber on the cutting board and slice it into chips.
    Even slices pack best, but a crooked one is still a pickle.


    Set a head
    of garlic on the board and press it to break it into cloves. Peel each clove;
    the papery skin comes right off.


    Set your jars on the dish towel, and the
    dill heads beside them.'
  - 'The brine


    Fill the cooking pot at the sink up to the first line. Pour
    vinegar in to the top line, about equal parts water and vinegar. Add two scoops
    of salt.


    Set the pot on a burner and turn the knob. Wait for a rolling
    boil. Once it has boiled, the brine stays ready, so you can move the pot to the
    hot pad beside the towel.'
  - 'Packing the jars


    In each jar put a head of dill and two cloves of garlic.
    More garlic never hurt anyone.


    Pack the slices in snug, a handful at a
    time.


    Pour the hot brine over them up to the line, leaving a little room
    under the lid, then press a lid on.'
  - 'The water bath


    Carry the canner to the sink and fill it with water, enough
    to cover the jars.


    Set it on a burner, lower the lidded jars into the rack,
    put the canner lid on, and turn the burner on. Let it boil for ten minutes. You
    can step away and do something else while it works.'
  - 'Cooling and keeping


    Take the canner lid off and lift each jar out onto
    the cooling towel. As they cool, listen for the ping: that''s the lid sealing.


    When
    they''ve sealed, take them to the table and put a label on each. Then carry them
    to the pantry shelf, two at a time, and stand back and admire them.


    A
    jar made just as the card says takes the blue ribbon at the county fair.'
  - 'Pickled garlic


    You will need about {cloves} peeled cloves for each pint
    (a couple of heads), a head of dill, and the peppercorn tin from the pantry.


    Break
    and peel the garlic on the cutting board. Set the jars and the peppercorn tin
    on the dish towel.'
  - 'Packing pickled garlic


    In each jar put a head of dill and a pinch of peppercorns,
    then fill it with peeled cloves, a handful at a time, until it''s full.


    Make
    the same brine as for pickles, pour it over the garlic to the line, and put on
    a lid. It goes through the same ten-minute water bath, and gets its own label
    at the table.'
  - 'Canned tomatoes


    You will need ripe tomatoes, about three for each pint,
    and the bottle of lemon juice from the pantry.


    Lay a tomato on the cutting
    board and cut it twice with the knife: once in half, then into quarters. Set
    the jars and the lemon juice on the dish towel.'
  - 'Packing tomatoes


    Press the wedges into each jar, a handful at a time,
    until it''s full. They give up their own juice as they go in, so there''s no
    brine to make.


    Add a spoonful of lemon juice to every jar, then a lid.
    Tomatoes take much longer in the water bath than pickles; leave them boiling
    while you do something else, or let them finish overnight.'
  - "Salsa\n\nYou will need about five ripe tomatoes, an onion and two jalape\xF1os
    for a pot, which makes four pints. A clove of garlic is nice too.\n\nQuarter
    the tomatoes and dice the onion and slice the peppers on the cutting board. Line them
    up at the back of the board, or empty the basket onto it, and they'll come to
    you one at a time."
  - 'Cooking the salsa


    Carry the cooking pot to the board and scrape everything
    in. Add a splash of vinegar and a scoop of salt, set it on a burner, and bring
    it to a good boil.


    Set the pot on the hot pad and ladle the salsa into
    empty jars, half an inch from the top. Lids on, then fifteen minutes in the water
    bath.'
